tion sort, but on an array that is already mostly sorted.\n\n    The gap sequence used here is a simple `n // 2`, then `gap //= 2` until gap is 0.\n\n    Args:\n        arr (list): A list of numbers (integers or floats) to be sorted.\n\n    Returns:\n        list: The same list `arr`, but with its elements sorted in ascending order.\n              Modifies the input list in-place.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # FR5: Handle empty list or a list with a single element correctly.\n    # If the list has 0 or 1 elements, it is already sorted.\n    if n <= 1:\n        return arr\n\n    # 5.2.1.3 - Initialize Gap: Start with a large gap.\n    # NFR1.1: Using the simple gap = n // 2 sequence.\n    gap = n // 2\n\n    # 5.2.1.3 - Outer Loop (Gap Reduction): Continue as long as gap > 0.\n    while gap > 0:\n        # 5.2.1.3 - Inner Loop (Sublist Sorting): Perform an insertion sort for elements\n        # that are `gap` distance apart.\n        # Iterate `i` from `gap` to `n - 1`.\n        for i in range(gap, n):\n            # 5.2.1.3 - i: Store the current element `arr[i]` in a `temp` variable.\n            temp = arr[i]\n\n            # 5.2.1.3 - ii: Initialize `j` for comparison.\n            j = i\n\n            # 5.2.1.3 - iii: Innermost Loop (Insertion Sort-like Comparison):\n            # Shift elements `gap` distance apart that are larger than `temp`\n            # to the right, creating a space for `temp`.\n            while j >= gap and arr[j - gap] > temp:\n                arr[j] = arr[j - gap]  # Shift `arr[j - gap]` to `arr[j]`\n                j -= gap               # Decrement `j` by `gap`\n\n            # 5.2.1.3 - iv: Place `temp` into its correct position within the sublist.\n            arr[j] = temp\n\n        # 5.2.1.3 - b: Reduce the `gap` for the next pass.\n        gap //= 2\n\n    # FR4: Return the sorted list `arr`.\n    return arr", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "Mbpp/429": [{"t