inimum possible elements from arr1's left partition.\n    #      We must take at least k - n elements from arr1 if k is large\n    #      and arr2 cannot contribute enough.\n    # high: maximum possible elements from arr1's left partition.\n    #       We can take at most k elements (if arr2 is empty or contributes few)\n    #       and at most m elements (total elements in arr1).\n    low = max(0, k - n)\n    high = min(k, m)\n\n    while low <= high:\n        # mid1 is the number of elements taken from arr1's left partition.\n        mid1 = (low + high) // 2\n        # mid2 is the number of elements taken from arr2's left partition.\n        # It's derived from k and mid1, ensuring mid1 + mid2 = k.\n        mid2 = k - mid1\n\n        # Determine the four critical elements around the partition:\n        # L1: Largest element in arr1's left partition.\n        # R1: Smallest element in arr1's right partition.\n        # L2: Largest element in arr2's left partition.\n        # R2: Smallest element in arr2's right partition.\n        # Use float('-inf') and float('inf') to handle boundary conditions\n        # when mid1 or mid2 is 0 or at array length.\n        L1 = arr1[mid1 - 1] if mid1 > 0 else float('-inf')\n        R1 = arr1[mid1] if mid1 < m else float('inf')\n        L2 = arr2[mid2 - 1] if mid2 > 0 else float('-inf')\n        R2 = arr2[mid2] if mid2 < n else float('inf')\n\n        # Check if partitions are correct:\n        # If L1 <= R2 and L2 <= R1, we found the correct partition.\n        # This means all elements in the left partitions (up to L1 and L2)\n        # are less than or equal to all elements in the right partitions\n        # (from R1 and R2). The k-th smallest element is the maximum\n        # of L1 and L2.\n        if L1 <= R2 and L2 <= R1:\n            return max(L1, L2)\n        # If L1 > R2, it means L1 is too large. We need to reduce elements from arr1's\n        # left partition, so we move high to mid1 - 1.\n        elif L1 > R2:\n      